## 7. Screen Operating System

The platform requires an explicit screen operating system. Screen work is not an informal byproduct of service analysis; it is a governed manual execution ladder with exact grouping, order, stop rules, and seal conditions.

| Screen Ladder State | Meaning | Entry Requirement | Exit Requirement |
|---|---|---|---|
| Candidate | Observed in donor or target evidence. | Screen or unit is discovered with source trace. | Classification decision exists. |
| Retained | Lawful to keep in active scope. | Keep/Merge/Convert/Internal decision explicit. | Canonical name, unit type, surface, and operation/journey linkage explicit. |
| Spec-Ready | Ready for exact spec writing. | Retained + master screen registry row complete. | Full screen spec created. |
| Design-Ready | Ready for detailed UX/UI design work. | Spec exists + purpose + CTA + states + variants. | Design review findings resolved for active bundle. |
| Build-Ready | Ready for implementation. | Group, lane, dependency, and component mapping explicit. | Implementation starts on canonical path only. |
| Review-Ready | Ready for structured QA/review. | Active build bundle implemented. | Review defects classified and resolved or BLOCKED. |
| Bind-Ready | Ready for canonical binding. | No screen ambiguity remains; API demand explicit. | Canonical chain row created. |
| Runtime-Ready | Ready for lawful runtime truth use. | Binding explicit; runtime source classified. | Proof class assigned. |
| Seal-Ready | Ready for closure consideration. | Verification, evidence, and blockers explicit. | Service sign-off consumes the screen state. |
| Sealed | Included in a sealed service scope. | Evidence-indexed sign-off passes. | Only superseded by explicit override. |

### Screen Grouping Law

- Every retained screen must belong to exactly one group and exactly one build step.
- Manual execution order must explicitly define review order, redesign order, preview order, implementation order, validation order, and seal order.
- Compression decisions are lawful only after registry completeness — never before.

